  "summary": "A ride through 1960 — highways, suburbs and the future as theme park.",
  "description": "General Motors' Futurama exhibit at the 1939 New York World's Fair — designed by Norman Bel Geddes — carried 26 million visitors on moving chairs over the world's largest animated model: a 35,000-square-foot miniature America of 1960, all fourteen-lane highways, cloverleaf interchanges and planned suburbs under unified light. It was propaganda for highway-building that largely came true, and it set the template for futurism-as-attraction: tomorrow as an amusement ride with scripted light and sound. Its sibling exhibits (Trylon & Perisphere, Democracity) fixed the fair's visual language — white geometry, upward arrows, unified night illumination — in American imagination.",
